## DESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Exploratory data analysis/descriptive statistics)
## DBsection(Summary statistics)
## Date(6/3/2002)
## Institution(The College of Idaho)
## Author(R Cruz, L Danielson, J Guild)
## MLT(stat-des-quartiles)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('The Basic Practice of Statistics')
## AuthorText1('David Moore')
## EditionText1('5e')
## Section1('2')
## Problem1('')
## TitleText2('Mathematical Statistics')
## AuthorText2('Wackerly, Mendenhall, Scheaffer')
## EditionText2('6')
## Section2('1.6')
## Problem2('22')
## KEYWORDS('statistics', 'skewed', 'mean', 'median','Measure', 'Quartile','measures','quartile')

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

@a = num_sort(map { random(20, 80) } (1 .. 27));

$k = random(1, 3, 2);    #Determine Q1 or Q3

$quartile = $k == 1 ? $a[6] : $a[20];

BEGIN_PGML
Here is a list of 27 scores on a Statistics midterm exam: 

[``` \begin{aligned}
[@ join(', ', @a[0..8]) @] \\
[@ join(', ', @a[9..17]) @] \\
[@ join(', ', @a[18..26]) @]\\
\end{aligned}
```]
Find [`Q_{[$k]}`]: [_]{$quartile}
END_PGML

$ph1  = $k == 1 ? 'first'       : 'third';
$ph2  = $k == 1 ? 'first'       : 'last';
@half = $k == 1 ? @a[ 0 .. 12 ] : @a[ 14 .. 26 ];

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
To find the [$ph1] quartile, we find the median of the [$ph2] half of the data and since there are an odd number of values, we don't include the middle one. That is we find the median of 

[``` [@ join(',', @half) @] ```]

And we take the middle number here or [`[$quartile]`].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();       # This should be the last executable line in the problem.